<p>In the latest development, Iran said the United States and Israel struck the Natanz nuclear facility, while Tehran also launched missiles toward the joint U.S.-U.K. base on Diego Garcia. Multiple reports said none of the missiles hit the base. President Trump said the United States was considering “winding down” military operations, even as thousands more Marines and additional warships were reportedly heading to the region. The contrast underscores the gap between de-escalation signals and the continuing military buildup. The war has now entered its fourth week after U.S. and Israeli strikes on Iran began on February 28, and it has spread across several fronts, including Israel, Iran, Lebanon, Iraq, and Gulf energy infrastructure. Iran said the Natanz strike caused no radioactive leak, and the International Atomic Energy Agency urged military restraint, offering one of the few steadying notes in an otherwise worsening regional confrontation. The broader stakes remain high. Key shipping through the Strait of Hormuz has been severely disrupted, energy facilities in Gulf states have come under attack, and several reports say more than 2,000 people have been killed since late February. At the same time, Washington has eased sanctions on some Iranian oil, a sign that policymakers are also trying to limit pressure on global energy markets even as the fighting continues.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/dsfkLC7YsPbt4SPT9SL_xve16So8oGIS-zCRyrUHn2GxcH4SQ9-bvaqgFPxI-VG8kDX-VVugViF4ql9MXOJjSM_dj3Zq4rS2tGPiwh_ddBdn8RgXlyLlHjrtRdOdQafPa-V_qN6mFu3XIVxYCQ6QF356ZKmBCrV4uMbJgwT6-5XBYltX3q62aMn8_UaA-rzE33xx85oclqKboSjs9gUY6lLqxzrz6Xq_EW-KmbWF3naUGPWxps7WdhcAba7Z3ZzsODq2tQXVjx4eS6jqug' alt='Israeli security personnel inspect a kindergarten hit after a strike near Tel Aviv on March 21.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Missile range: The reported launch toward Diego Garcia, roughly 4,000 kilometers from Iran, raised fresh questions about whether Tehran has longer-range missiles than it has publicly acknowledged.</li><li>Regional spillover: As the conflict pushed deeper into the Gulf, Saudi Arabia and the United Arab Emirates said they were responding to incoming missile and drone threats.</li><li>Oil market strain: Brent crude rose 3.3% to $112.19 a barrel, while major U.S. stock indexes fell as investors weighed the risk of prolonged supply disruptions and weaker global growth.</li><li>Lebanon front: Israeli strikes on Hezbollah-linked targets in Lebanon have killed more than 1,000 people, according to Lebanon’s health ministry as cited in reporting on the widening war.</li><li>Shipping security: Britain said it was working with partners on a plan to safeguard international shipping in the Strait of Hormuz after backing U.S. use of U.K. bases for strikes on Iranian missile sites.</li></ul><blockquote>We are getting very close to meeting our objectives as we consider winding down our great Military efforts in the Middle East. - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Iranian Atomic Energy Organization: The agency said the United States and Israel targeted Natanz but reported no leakage of radioactive materials from the site. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/u-s-israel-strike-on-irans-natanz-nuclear-facility/article70768830.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>IAEA: The U.N. nuclear watchdog urged “military restraint” after the reported strike on Natanz. (<a href='https://www.rferl.org/a/trump-winding-down-war-iran-israel-oil-sanctions/33711926.html'>Radio Free Europe/Radio Liberty</a>)</li><li>U.K. government: London condemned Iran’s strike on Diego Garcia and said it was coordinating with partners on a plan to protect shipping through the Strait of Hormuz. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/israel-iran-launch-fresh-attacks-donald-trump-considers-winding-down-middle-east-operations/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li>President Donald Trump: The president said U.S. objectives were close to being 